  "course_description": "Special Topics course.",
  "course_notes": "(Same as AAS 285 2, ENVS 285 2, and HIST 296 1) (Complete Topic - Wade in the Water: Race, Water, and Environmental Health Policy in Black America) (This course examines the history of water and environmental health in Black communities in the United States. The course focuses on flooding, dampness, mold, housing, hurricanes, insurance, property, and disaster recovery to understand how water-related environmental conditions have produced different health outcomes across race. Atlanta and New Orleans serve as the two primary sites for the course. In Atlanta, we examine the longer history of flooding, stormwater, sewer infrastructure, damp housing, mold, respiratory health, homeownership, and neighborhood development. New Orleans allows us to examine many of these same issues through hurricanes and large-scale flooding, particularly Hurricanes Betsy, Katrina, and Ida.)",
